1
00:00:00,240 --> 00:00:03,120
我们现在正处于个人代理的浪潮中，甚至有些过载了。
We are officially drowning in personal agents.

2
00:00:03,342 --> 00:00:12,004
无论是 Muse、Grokbot、OpenClaw、Hermes、DotsNow、Instinct，还是 Anthropic 最终推出的任何产品，
Between Muse and Grokbot and OpenClaw and Hermes and DotsNow and Instinct and whatever anthropic inevitably launches,

3
00:00:12,320 --> 00:00:15,120
这种形态现在无处不在。
this is a form factor that is absolutely everywhere.

4
00:00:15,310 --> 00:00:25,040
考虑到要充分利用这些个人代理所需的上下文、设置、工具权限和账户权限，切换成本可能会相当高。
And given how much context and setup and tool access and account access is going to be required to get the most out of these personal agents, the cost of switching could be kind of high.

5
00:00:25,486 --> 00:00:33,860
鉴于此，今天我们将提供一份指南，根据一系列不同标准来选择个人代理，帮助你确定最适合你的那一个。
Given all that, today we are looking at a guide to how to choose a personal agent based on a set of different criteria that should help you hone in on which one is best for you.

6
00:00:34,862 --> 00:00:39,908
AI Daily Brief 是一个关于 AI 领域最重要的动态和讨论的每日播客和视频节目。
The AI Daily Brief is a daily podcast and video about the most important news and discussions in AI.

7
00:00:41,230 --> 00:00:43,600
今天我们要聊的是个人代理。
Today we are talking about personal agents.

8
00:00:43,822 --> 00:00:45,520
这是我对此事的看法。
And here's my shtick on this.

9
00:00:45,806 --> 00:00:55,044
我认为现在还处于早期阶段，对于这种大家都在竞相实现的特定形态最终会成为主流，保持一定的怀疑态度是合理的。
I think it's early enough that having some amount of skepticism that this particular form factor that everyone is racing to implement for you will ultimately be where things land.

10
00:00:55,534 --> 00:01:05,519
然而，你很可能至少会拥有一个高度互联的代理，并集成到你的邮件账户、Slack 或 Teams 中，
However, the idea that you are likely to have at least one highly connected agent integrated with your email accounts, your Slack or Teams,

11
00:01:05,742 --> 00:01:11,652
甚至可能还会接入你的财务账户等信息，这正变得越来越普遍。
and even potentially having access to things like your financial accounts is going to be increasingly normal.

12
00:01:12,142 --> 00:01:20,480
所以我的观点是，即使你不确定这是否完全适合你，我认为还是值得花点时间去尝试一下，
My argument then is that even if you're not sure that this is exactly a fit for you, I think it's worth carving out some experimentation time

13
00:01:20,640 --> 00:01:25,956
看看使用个人代理会如何影响你的职业或个人生活。
to see if and how using a personal agent impacts anything in your professional or personal life.

14
00:01:26,446 --> 00:01:32,228
这并不意味着你必须给它所有权限才能真正体会到它的作用，但确实需要投入一些实际的时间和练习。
Now that does not mean you have to give it access to everything to get a real sense of it, but it does mean putting in some actual time and reps.

15
00:01:32,800 --> 00:01:40,240
但你的时间很宝贵，而且非常有限。那么，你会选择哪种个人代理来尝试呢？
But your time is precious, and there is too little of it. So which personal agent are you going to choose to experiment with?

16
00:01:40,750 --> 00:01:43,600
今天的节目核心就是这个问题，但我们将把它分为三个部分。
Today's show is all about that question, but we're going to divide it into three parts.

17
00:01:43,982 --> 00:01:50,880
我将介绍一个高层级的思考框架，并分享一个我制作的互动测试，供你在听完本集后自行使用。
I'll go through a high-level framework for thinking about it, share a little interactive quiz that I built that you can use yourself after you listen to this episode.

18
00:01:51,182 --> 00:01:58,916
但在那之前，我想读一些 Ethan Moloch 教授在他 One Useful Thing 博客上最新发布的文章片段，题目叫《点与集群》。
But before we do that, I want to read some excerpts from Professor Ethan Moloch's most recent post on his One Useful Thing blog, which is called The Dot and the Swarm.

19
00:01:59,310 --> 00:02:07,204
这是一篇关于这种个人代理形态的思考，以及为什么即使是像 Ethan 这样密切关注动态的人，也可能误判 AI 的走向。
It's a meditation on this personal agent form factor, and why even someone who watches things as closely as Ethan does can miss where AI is headed.

20
00:02:07,726 --> 00:02:15,364
Ethan 写道：在写这篇 Substack 的几年里，我通常认为自己很好地预判了 AI 的发展方向和速度。
Ethan writes, I generally think I have done a good job anticipating the direction and pace of AI over the few years I've been writing this Substack.

21
00:02:16,046 --> 00:02:19,684
但我认为我最近在某件相当大的事情上搞错了。
But I think I recently got something fairly large wrong.

22
00:02:20,142 --> 00:02:26,720
在过去的一年里，我一直在发帖讨论我怀疑人类在与代理协作时必须扮演管理者的角色，
In the last year, I've been posting about how I suspected that humans would have to approach working with agents as a manager,

23
00:02:26,880 --> 00:02:31,396
决定如何将工作分配给代理，并规定这些代理应该如何组织。
deciding how to delegate work to agents, and specifying how those agents should be organized.

24
00:02:31,758 --> 00:02:39,460
我原以为让代理有效地作为一个群体工作需要精心的构建，类似于建立一家公司，而且这需要很长时间去摸索。
I thought that getting agents to work effectively as a group would take careful construction, akin to building a company, and that this would take time to figure out.

25
00:02:40,078 --> 00:02:40,516
并没有。
Nope.

26
00:02:41,006 --> 00:02:42,880
我成了「苦涩的教训」的受害者。
I fell prey to the bitter lesson.

27
00:02:43,086 --> 00:02:52,708
这是一个反复被证明的残酷事实：我们认为需要复杂的人类规则和思考的事情，可以通过更强大的机器学习系统和更多的 AI 来暴力破解。
The hard truth, learned over and over again, that things that we thought required elaborate human rules and thinking can be solved with the brute force of better machine learning systems and more AI.

28
00:02:53,134 --> 00:02:57,280
「苦涩的教训」在所有采用 AI 的初创公司和企业中无处不在。
The bitter lesson is everywhere among AI startups and companies adopting AI.

29
00:02:57,550 --> 00:03:05,508
人们投入了大量精力去构建复杂的计算机系统，以便在正确的时间给 AI 提供正确的信息，但 AI 系统已经学会了自己去寻找信息。
A huge amount of effort went into building elaborate computer systems to feed AIs the right information at the right time, but AI systems have learned to seek out information themselves.

30
00:03:05,934 --> 00:03:07,524
同样的情况也发生在提示词工程上。
The same thing happened to prompting.

31
00:03:07,790 --> 00:03:12,640
人们构建了复杂的模板和提示词链，引导 AI 一步步完成任务。
People built elaborate templates and chains of prompts that walk the AI through a task one step at a time.

32
00:03:12,974 --> 00:03:19,076
然而新模型被证明更擅长自行规划步骤，而且我们的研究显示，手动规划步骤的价值要低得多。
Then newer models turned out to be better at planning the steps themselves, and as our research shows, planning steps have much less value.

33
00:03:19,662 --> 00:03:26,400
作为一名培训经理人并发表过管理相关研究的人，我原以为管理代理会有所不同。
As somebody who teaches managers and has published research on management, I guess I believed that managing agents would be different.

34
00:03:26,734 --> 00:03:30,628
人类一直在研究管理工作，但直到现在还没完全搞清楚。
Humans have been working on management for a very long time without fully figuring it out.

35
00:03:30,894 --> 00:03:34,628
这看起来像是某种至少在一段时间内需要由人类来设计的环节。
It seemed like the kind of thing that would need to be designed by people at least for a while.

36
00:03:34,926 --> 00:03:38,756
结果证明，组织工作只是 AI 可以学会做的又一件事。
It turns out that organizing work is just one more thing AI can learn to do.

37
00:03:39,438 --> 00:03:41,280
这就引出了 Dots 和 Muse。
Which brings us to Dots and Muse.

38
00:03:41,934 --> 00:03:47,040
目前 App Store 排行第一的 App 是 Meta 的 Muse，这是一个承诺能为你处理工作的个人代理。
The number one app in the App Store right now is Meta's Muse, a personal agent that promises to do work for you.

39
00:03:47,406 --> 00:03:50,080
OpenAI 现在也发布了一款名为 Dots 的竞争工具。
OpenAI has now released a competitor tool called Dots.

40
00:03:50,478 --> 00:03:51,280
他们并非孤军奋战。
They aren't alone.

41
00:03:51,662 --> 00:03:56,228
SpaceX 的 Grokbot、Instinct 和 Gemini Spark 基本都在做类似的事情。
SpaceX's Grokbot, Instinct, and Gemini Spark all do similar things, more or less.

42
00:03:56,750 --> 00:04:01,476
所有这些代理都从今年早些时候的一个现象中汲取灵感，即 OpenClaw。
All of these agents draw inspiration from a phenomenon you might remember from earlier this year, OpenClaw.

43
00:04:01,966 --> 00:04:09,840
OpenClaw 及其继任者（我称之为 Clawlikes）的核心理念是让 AI 代理获得电脑访问权限，并连接到你的账户、邮件
The idea of OpenClaw and its successors, which I will call Clawlikes, is that they give an AI agent access to a computer and connect to your accounts, emails,

44
00:04:10,000 --> 00:04:11,040
财务记录等。
financial records, etc.

45
00:04:11,342 --> 00:04:14,852
它们会实时分析并响应这些数据，甚至在你不在场的时候也是如此。
They analyze and react to that data in real time, even when you aren't looking.

46
00:04:15,342 --> 00:04:23,520
诀窍在于你像对待真人一样与模型交流，在 Slack、SMS 或 WhatsApp 上给它发信息，而它也会像真人一样主动联系你。
The trick is that you talk to the model like you would a person, sending it messages on Slack or SMS or WhatsApp, and it also proactively reaches out to you like a person would.

47
00:04:23,918 --> 00:04:26,480
对于 Dots，你甚至可以和你的代理进行通话。
For dots, you can actually jump on a call with your agent as well.

48
00:04:26,734 --> 00:04:30,116
你基本上得到了一个极具耐心、能为你照应的个人助理。
You basically get an infinitely patient personal assistant that looks out for you.

49
00:04:30,606 --> 00:04:35,600
我发现，它们越来越多地在帮我纠正错误，而不是让我去找出它们的错误。
Increasingly, I have discovered that they are finding my mistakes rather than having me identify theirs.

50
00:04:36,142 --> 00:04:44,000
Ethan 随后举了几个例子，包括他为所在城镇申请许可证时，他的代理注意到他填错了一项内容；还有 Muse
Ethan then gives a couple of examples, including having sent out a permit for the town that he's in, and his agent noticing that he had filled something in wrong, and then Muse

51
00:04:44,160 --> 00:04:50,084
注意到他的一张航空公司代金券即将过期，并写了一份便条联系航空公司要求延期。
noticing that an airline credit that he had was about to expire, and writing up a note to contact the airline to request an extension.

52
00:04:50,862 --> 00:04:57,540
Ethan 继续说，很容易用它们能做到的功能列表来评判这些代理，比如预订旅行或取消订阅。
Ethan continues, it's tempting to judge these agents by the list of things they can do, like booking travel or canceling subscriptions.

53
00:04:57,838 --> 00:05:00,836
但我认为更重要的一点是，你不再需要告诉它们什么。
I think the more important thing is what you no longer have to tell them.

54
00:05:01,294 --> 00:05:03,396
你不需要输入大量的上下文信息。
You don't need to type in tons of context.

55
00:05:03,726 --> 00:05:05,412
AI 会从你的消息中学习这些内容。
The AI learns it from your messages.

56
00:05:05,742 --> 00:05:08,484
你不需要给它们制定计划，它们会自己制定计划。
You don't have to give them a plan, they develop plans themselves.

57
00:05:08,910 --> 00:05:09,968
它们能搞定。
They figure it out.

58
00:05:10,350 --> 00:05:12,368
如果只是一个代理，这已经足够令人印象深刻了。
That would be impressive enough if it were one agent.

59
00:05:12,622 --> 00:05:16,996
但真正改变我关于管理的想法是，当有成千上万个这样的代理时会发生什么。
What actually changed my mind about management is what happens when there are thousands of them.

60
00:05:17,742 --> 00:05:18,368
就是集群。
Swarms.

61
00:05:18,862 --> 00:05:25,892
9月8日，OpenAI 发布了 Clay Institute 千年难题之一——Navier-Stokes Existence and Smoothness Problem 的证明。
On September 8th, OpenAI announced a proof for one of the Clay Institute's Millennium Prize problems, the Navier-Stokes Existence and Smoothness Problem.

62
00:05:26,254 --> 00:05:30,116
这是数学领域最著名的未解难题之一，悬赏高达 100 万美元。
It is among the most famous open problems in mathematics, with a $1 million prize.

63
00:05:30,670 --> 00:05:34,208
但 OpenAI 显然是仅用 AI 在 88 小时内就解决了它。
But OpenAI apparently solved it using AI alone for 88 hours.

64
00:05:34,574 --> 00:05:37,060
让我感兴趣的不是数学本身，而是它是如何做到的。
What interests me is less the math than how it was done.

65
00:05:37,742 --> 00:05:40,048
OpenAI 推出了一个现在被称为「Swarm」的东西。
OpenAI launched what is now being called a Swarm.

66
00:05:40,288 --> 00:05:42,528
名字起得很烂，但似乎这就是我们不得不接受的现状。
Terrible name, but it appears to be what we are stuck with.

67
00:05:42,862 --> 00:05:45,444
这是一个由先进模型驱动、包含数千个智能体的群体。
A group of thousands of agents powered by an advanced model.

68
00:05:45,966 --> 00:05:52,288
OpenAI 给不同群体的智能体分配了不同的问题，并随着智能体的进展将重心转向 Navier Stokes 问题。
OpenAI gave groups of agents different problems to solve, then shifted the effort to Navier Stokes as the agents made progress.

69
00:05:52,622 --> 00:05:56,196
公司设定了目标，但其协调结构却异常精简。
The company set the goals, but its coordination structure was remarkably thin.

70
00:05:56,654 --> 00:06:00,388
只有几个小组、一次方向转变，以及 Codex 在它们之间传递最佳创意。
A few groups, one change of direction, and Codex passing the best ideas between them.

71
00:06:00,910 --> 00:06:04,288
在每个小组内部，智能体们自主地互相传递想法。
Within each group, the agents transmitted ideas back and forth on their own.

72
00:06:04,622 --> 00:06:09,648
这些智能体发送了大约 270 万条消息，并在 88 小时后得出了结果。
The agents sent about 2.7 million messages, reaching their result after 88 hours.

73
00:06:10,062 --> 00:06:13,796
这种类型的协作在 Hugging Face 事件中以一种更阴暗的形式出现过。
This same type of coordination in its darker form occurred during the hugging face incident.

74
00:06:14,126 --> 00:06:21,764
AI 自发组织成团队，并以从未计划过的方式互相沟通，但它们利用这种协作去攻击一个网站，而不是解决问题。
AI self-organized into teams and communicated with each other in ways that were never planned, but used that coordination to attack a website rather than solve a problem.

75
00:06:22,254 --> 00:06:26,048
在我的旧模型下，想想管理这类工作需要付出多少代价。
Under my old model, think about what managing this kind of work would have required.

76
00:06:26,414 --> 00:06:28,848
如果有一个不明确的问题，有 10,000 名员工？
10,000 workers in an unspecified problem?

77
00:06:29,134 --> 00:06:30,448
你该如何告诉他们该做什么？
How would you tell them what to do?

78
00:06:30,734 --> 00:06:34,208
人类经理如何判断这 270 万条消息中哪一条是有意义的？
How would a human manager decide which of 2.7 million messages mattered?

79
00:06:34,382 --> 00:06:36,068
他们又该如何互相协作？
How would they coordinate with each other?

80
00:06:36,590 --> 00:06:37,956
而这个 Swarm 搞定了这一切。
The swarm figured it out.

81
00:06:38,478 --> 00:06:44,388
我没有 10,000 个智能体，但我现在经常看到 OpenAI 的 Codex 和 Claude Code 根据需要调用智能体。
I don't have 10,000 agents, but I now regularly see OpenAI's codecs and Claude Code using agents as needed.

82
00:06:44,814 --> 00:06:51,728
举个例子，当我给配备了 GPT-6 Astra Ultra 的 Codex 输入提示词：为我的下一个 One Useful Thing 帖子构思创意并选出一个时，
As an example, when I gave Codecs with GPT-6 Astra Ultra the prompt, brainstorm ideas for my next one useful thing post and select one,

83
00:06:51,982 --> 00:07:00,368
要求从尽可能多的角度生成创意，并从事实、读者以及其他类似报道的角度来评估它们。AI 随即
generate ideas from as many angles as possible, and evaluate them from both factual and reader perspectives, as well as other publications doing similar coverage, the AI spun

84
00:07:00,558 --> 00:07:01,380
启动了三个智能体。
up three agents.

85
00:07:01,774 --> 00:07:07,428
当我用几句话勾勒出三个团队——构思者、研究员和一组读者时，它给出了 13 个结果。
When I sketched three teams in a few sentences, brainstormers, researchers, and a panel of readers, I got 13.

86
00:07:07,918 --> 00:07:09,968
注意看我需要做的组织工作是多么少。
Notice how little organizing I had to do.

87
00:07:10,382 --> 00:07:15,716
选择 ultra 模式会告诉模型它可以进行委派，我提供了一个框架，但剩下的全交给 AI 了。
Selecting ultra mode tells the model it can delegate, and I provided a framework, but the rest was up to the AI.

88
00:07:16,206 --> 00:07:18,436
这就是将「苦涩的教训」应用到组织架构上。
This is the bitter lesson applied to the org chart.

89
00:07:18,798 --> 00:07:25,124
我原本认为需要人类精心设计数年才能解决的组织问题，很大程度上被那些更擅长组织工作的模型解决了。
The organizational problem I thought would take years of careful human design was largely solved by models that are better at organizing.

90
00:07:25,646 --> 00:07:30,276
但值得思考的是，为什么对智能体来说，组织工作竟然比我们要容易得多。
But it's worth asking why organizing turned out to be so much easier for agents than it is for us.

91
00:07:30,734 --> 00:07:35,328
我们所谓的很多管理工作，其实是为了解决由“人组成的组织”所带来的问题。
A lot of what we call management exists to solve problems that come from organizations being made of people.

92
00:07:35,662 --> 00:07:38,884
人有自己的目标，而这些并不总是组织的共同目标。
People have their own goals, and those aren't always the goals of organizations.

93
00:07:39,278 --> 00:07:46,116
我们称之为「委托人代理问题」，从奖金到管理架构，很多组织的机制都是为了解决这个问题。
We call this the principal agent problem, and a lot of the machinery of organizations, from bonuses to management structures, is based around solving it.

94
00:07:46,446 --> 00:07:48,708
此外还有一些非常人性化的问题。
And there are other very human problems as well.

95
00:07:49,230 --> 00:07:53,668
信息分散在每个人的脑子里，而且人们往往不愿分享或者忘了分享。
Information is scattered across people's heads, and people are often reluctant to share it or forget to.

96
00:07:53,902 --> 00:07:55,524
沟通也是有成本的。
Communication is expensive too.

97
00:07:55,854 --> 00:08:00,900
经理能带的人数有限，因此，在延期的软件项目中增加人手，往往只会让项目更晚完成。
Managers can only oversee so many people, thus, adding people to a late software project famously makes it later.

98
00:08:01,486 --> 00:08:04,448
管理在某种程度上是围绕人类的局限性建立的。
Management is in part built around human limitations.

99
00:08:04,782 --> 00:08:06,688
而代理（Agents）面临这些问题的数量要少得多。
Agents have far fewer of these problems.

100
00:08:06,958 --> 00:08:09,060
它们不会为了晋升而搞权术，也不会护食。
They don't angle for promotions or protect their turf.

101
00:08:09,488 --> 00:08:10,820
它们甚至不需要开会。
They don't even have meetings.

102
00:08:11,374 --> 00:08:17,316
即使在 Hugging Face 那次出状况的地方，那个集群也基本没有典型的组织病态。
Even at Hugging Face, where things went badly wrong, the swarm was largely free of classic organizational pathologies.

103
00:08:17,614 --> 00:08:21,968
这些代理不会搭别人的便车，有些甚至会为了团队目标牺牲自己的分数。
The agents didn't free ride on each other's work, and some sacrificed their own scores for the group.

104
00:08:22,318 --> 00:08:24,740
那些解决 Navier Stokes 方程的代理并不在乎功劳。
The agents that solved Navier Stokes didn't want credit.

105
00:08:24,974 --> 00:08:27,648
但这并不意味着 AI 没有「委托人代理问题」。
That doesn't mean AI has no principal agent problem.

106
00:08:27,982 --> 00:08:31,684
正如 Hugging Face 事件所示，集群与我们之间的问题正日益增多。
As the Hugging Face incident showed, there are increasingly problems between the Swarm and us.

107
00:08:32,206 --> 00:08:41,727
OpenAI 本周搁置了它的下一款模型 GPT-6 Astra，因为在测试中它未经许可就采取行动并错误报告了操作内容，这是典型的「委托人代理问题」。
OpenAI shelved its next model, GPT 61 Astra, this week because in testing it acted without permission and misreported what it had done, a textbook example of the principal agent problem.

108
00:08:42,350 --> 00:08:44,416
这些并不意味着代理能做所有事情。
None of this means agents can do everything.

109
00:08:44,718 --> 00:08:53,296
AI 目前仍过于受限，无法替代大量的体力劳动，而且我不确定自组织代理在处理占据组织大部分时间的那些冗长且枯燥的工作时表现如何。
AI is still too limited to substitute for large amounts of human work, and I don't know how well self-organizing agents handle the long, unglamorous work that fills most of an organization's time.

110
00:08:53,678 --> 00:08:58,576
此外，Hugging Face 的事件提醒我们，自组织系统可能会走向意想不到的方向。
Plus, the Hugging Face incident is a reminder that self-organizing systems can head in unexpected directions.

111
00:08:58,862 --> 00:09:01,476
但我不再认为组织代理是难点。
But I no longer think organizing agents is the hard part.

112
00:09:01,806 --> 00:09:02,896
这可能是个好消息。
This may be good news.

113
00:09:03,182 --> 00:09:11,616
我原以为公司需要为机器重建管理体系，构建由纯代理组成的复杂替代结构，甚至可能以牺牲人类角色和组织为代价。
I assumed companies would need to rebuild management for machines, constructing elaborate alternate structures populated solely by agents, often at the expense of human roles and organizations.

114
00:09:11,982 --> 00:09:19,844
但大部分管理工作是为了解决代理并不面临的问题，而且代理正越来越多地在人类使用的那些混乱系统中工作，即使是处理模糊的任务。
But much of management exists to solve problems agents don't have, and agents increasingly work through the same messy systems people do, even on ambiguous tasks.

115
00:09:20,238 --> 00:09:25,540
这表明只要人类能引导它们走向正确的方向，将它们整合进公司可能比预想的要容易。
That suggests they may be easier to integrate into firms than expected, as long as humans are guiding them in the right direction.

116
00:09:26,030 --> 00:09:31,136
如果做得出色且代理与我们的需求对齐，这可能意味着人们的工作量会增加，而不是减少。
Done well and with agents that are properly aligned to our needs, this could mean more work for people, not less.

117
00:09:31,502 --> 00:09:34,896
当组织成本高昂时，组织只会尝试他们有足够人手去完成的事情。
When organizing is expensive, organizations only attempt what they can staff.

118
00:09:35,118 --> 00:09:37,776
当成本降低时，值得尝试的事情清单就会变长。
When it gets cheap, the list of things worth attempting can grow.

119
00:09:38,222 --> 00:09:43,876
在 Navier Stokes 的实验中，代理负责组织工作，而人类决定让它们指向哪里，并在过程中不断重新评估。
In the Navier Stokes run, the agents did the organizing, but people decided where to point them, reassessing as the process continued.

120
00:09:44,334 --> 00:09:52,516
你可以争论 OpenAI 是否让它们指对了方向，但这种分工本身看起来是正确的，至少目前如此。这是来自 Ethan 的又一个非常深刻的帖子。
You can argue about whether OpenAI pointed them at the right thing, but the division itself seems right, at least for now. So another great thoughtful post from Ethan here.

121
00:09:52,816 --> 00:09:57,796
这并不是节目的核心内容，但我对这个问题的基本看法和 Ethan 的描述一致。
It's not really the subject of the show, but my base case for this is basically what Ethan describes.

122
00:09:58,222 --> 00:10:06,020
因为代理在融入现有系统方面比我们预想的要强，我认为我们更有可能看到的是，而不是现有的组织架构被彻底颠覆，
That because agents are better than we thought at integrating into the existing system, I think we're likely to see, rather than the existing org chart totally upended,

123
00:10:06,416 --> 00:10:08,804
而是组织架构中的每个环节都能达到预期。
more expected from every part of that org chart.

124
00:10:09,390 --> 00:10:16,656
我以前提到过「无限待办事项」这个概念，就是指那些理论上可以完成、但人们心知肚明的海量工作，
I've referred to a concept of an infinite backlog in the past, where there's this never-ending amount of work that could theoretically be done, but which people understand,

125
00:10:16,846 --> 00:10:20,036
其中有些部分因为太遥远而根本没机会去做。
some parts of which you just won't get to because it's too far out.

126
00:10:20,654 --> 00:10:27,936
我认为组织内部引入代理的实际效果是，让每个人和整个组织的这些「无限待办事项」
I think the practical effect of agents inside the organization is going to have every part of people's infinite backlog and the organization's infinite backlog

127
00:10:28,016 --> 00:10:30,500
都有可能变成我们真正能完成的工作。
become expected to actually be work that we get done.

128
00:10:31,150 --> 00:10:35,296
事实上，我认为我们在使用代理时会遇到的很多问题并不是大家都会丢掉工作，
I think, in fact, that a lot of the problems that we're going to run into with agents are not everyone losing their jobs,

129
00:10:35,376 --> 00:10:40,336
而是因为很难关掉它并决定暂时把某件事当作已完成，
but everyone having too much work because it's even harder to turn off and say it's okay to treat something as done for now,

130
00:10:40,496 --> 00:10:43,780
因为即使你不在忙，也可以随时增加更多代理来继续处理这些任务。
when you could always just spin up more agents to keep working on it even when you're not.

131
00:10:44,718 --> 00:10:50,628
但这一切都建立在一个前提上：我们每个人都会以深入且稳健的方式使用代理。
But all this rests on the idea that each of us individually is going to be using agents in a deep and robust way.

132
00:10:50,990 --> 00:10:53,412
这就带回到关于「个人代理」的问题上了。
Which brings us back to this question of personal agents.

133
00:10:53,742 --> 00:10:57,412
所以我们要深入探讨一下，开始尝试使用这些代理，但该选哪一个呢？
So we're gonna dive in, we're going to start experimenting with agents, but which one?

134
00:10:57,966 --> 00:10:59,216
在开始之前先提一下。
One shout-out before we begin.

135
00:10:59,376 --> 00:11:04,656
用于制作这部分内容以及后续测试的大量数据都来自 Every。
A lot of the data that was used to build the rest of the presentation, and the quiz that you can do after, comes from Every.

136
00:11:05,006 --> 00:11:09,456
他们对八种不同的代理在大量维度上进行了横向对比，
They put together a side-by-side comparison of eight different agents across a ton of different dimensions,

137
00:11:09,678 --> 00:11:13,828
而这些正是帮助我制作本节内容的关键原始信息。
which is exactly the sort of raw information that an agent needs to help me build out this show.

138
00:11:14,382 --> 00:11:23,936
因此我们使用的这八个代理，部分原因是因为它们包含在 Every 的图表中：OpenAI 的 Dots，Google 的 Gemini Spark，SpaceX AI 的 Grockbot，Noose
So the eight agents that we're using, in part because these are the ones that were included in Every's chart, are Dots from OpenAI, Google's Gemini Spark, SpaceX AI's Grockbot, Noose

139
00:11:24,096 --> 00:11:29,252
Research 的 Hermes Agent，Meta 的 Muse，还有 OpenClaw、Poke 和 Instinct。
Research's Hermes Agent, Meta's Muse, and then OpenClaw, Poke, and Instinct as well.

140
00:11:29,646 --> 00:11:34,816
现在，有一点可能没法帮你做决定，那就是直接的功能对比。
Now, one thing that won't be all that useful in helping you decide is a direct feature comparison.

141
00:11:35,182 --> 00:11:37,444
这是因为目前存在非常明显的功能趋同。
That is because there is a very clear feature convergence.

142
00:11:37,710 --> 00:11:45,412
它们几乎都能连接你的应用和服务，绝大多数都能代替你使用浏览器，而且它们都有不同程度的可定制控制选项。
All of them more or less connect to your apps and services, mostly all of them can use a web browser for you, and all of them have various levels of controls that you can customize.

143
00:11:45,966 --> 00:11:48,708
那么，有没有更好的方法来判断哪一个最适合你？
So, what are some better ways to determine which might be the fit for you?

144
00:11:49,454 --> 00:11:52,736
有几个问题我觉得最为关键。
There are a handful of questions that stand out to me as most important.

145
00:11:53,070 --> 00:11:54,756
第一个就是最重要的那个。
The first is the big one.

146
00:11:55,214 --> 00:11:58,372
这是主要用于工作，还是用于个人事务？
Is this primarily for work or for personal matters?

147
00:11:58,830 --> 00:12:02,084
当然，这些代理中没有一个会说它只属于其中一类。
Now, none of these agents would say it's only for one or the other.

148
00:12:02,414 --> 00:12:05,764
事实上，它们的存在本身就在模糊这些界限。
In fact, their very existence sort of blurs these lines.

149
00:12:06,222 --> 00:12:08,896
这些代理在意的是「你」这个上下文。
These agents care about the context that is you.

150
00:12:09,198 --> 00:12:16,964
所以无论你给它访问哪些内容和系统，无论是个人还是工作的，它在为你办事时都会把这些因素全部考虑进去。
And so whatever context and systems you give it access to, whether they are personal or work, it's going to take that all into consideration as it does things for you.

151
00:12:17,550 --> 00:12:21,776
不过，这些代理系统确实在定位上略有不同。
Still, different of these agent systems are certainly pointed a little bit more or less in one direction or the other.

152
00:12:22,190 --> 00:12:28,976
Meta 推出的 Muse，不出意料地，考虑到他们本质上是一家消费类公司，更偏向于个人用途的场景，
Muse from Meta, unsurprisingly, given that they are at heart a consumer company, is a little bit more aimed at personal types of use cases,

153
00:12:29,056 --> 00:12:35,588
而 Grokbot 则更倾向于工作用途，或者至少从功能逻辑来看，它被更多人用于工作。
where something like Grokbot is a little bit more aimed at work, or at least based on how it functions, is being adopted by more people for work.

154
00:12:36,110 --> 00:12:40,464
OpenAI 显然是一家可能同时涵盖这两种用例的公司。
OpenAI is certainly a company that finds itself as potentially having both of these use cases.

155
00:12:40,910 --> 00:12:47,492
拥有 12 亿周活跃用户，肯定有很多消费者想要在个人代理场景中获得帮助。
With 1.2 billion weekly active users, there is no shortage of consumers who might want help with personal agentic use cases.

156
00:12:47,726 --> 00:12:52,900
不过从目前 Dot 的设计来看，我觉得它更偏向于工作用途。
Although as DOTs is designed now, it seems to me to be a bit more aimed at the work use case.

157
00:12:53,390 --> 00:12:56,944
当然，它仅在付费账户中提供这一事实也指向了那个方向。
Certainly the fact that it's only available in paid accounts points in that direction.

158
00:12:57,390 --> 00:13:01,636
所以，是工作还是个人用途是第一个可以帮助你决定哪个最合适的问题。
So worker personal use cases is the first question which can help you decide which is the best fit.

159
00:13:02,126 --> 00:13:09,988
这里也值得注意的是，这个问题的整个前提是假设你主要依赖于系统自带的现有用户体验和原生集成。
It's also worth noting here that the entire premise of the question assumes that you're mostly relying on the existing UX and native integrations that come with a system.

160
00:13:10,446 --> 00:13:13,316
例如，Dot 与 Slack 和 Teams 有原生集成。
For example, dot being natively integrated with Slack and Teams.

161
00:13:13,584 --> 00:13:20,784
然而，当你使用像 OpenClaw 或 Hermes 这样的工具时，你显然有更大的灵活性来让它满足你的任何需求。
However, when you're using something like OpenClaw or Hermes, you obviously have a lot more flexibility to make it be for whatever you want.

162
00:13:21,166 --> 00:13:24,464
因此，如果你走向那个方向，可能需要使用其他的标准。
And so you might need to use other criteria if you're headed in that direction.

163
00:13:24,750 --> 00:13:33,664
此外，虽然目前判断一个代理偏向工作还是个人最简单的方法是看它是否支持 iMessage 和 WhatsApp 等个人通讯系统，
Also, while right now, one of the best ways to tell whether an agent leans work or personal is whether it uses personal messaging systems like iMessage and WhatsApp,

164
00:13:33,824 --> 00:13:41,984
或者 Slack 和 Teams 等工作通讯系统，但我如果再过六个月这些代理还没能在所有通讯系统中运行，我都会感到非常惊讶。
or work messaging systems like Slack and Teams, but I would be very surprised if in six months all of these agents didn't just work in all of the messaging systems.

165
00:13:42,350 --> 00:13:46,212
所以这种区分工作或个人的能力可能不会持续太久。
So this ability to indicate whether it's more work or personal may not last very long.

166
00:13:46,702 --> 00:13:51,904
现在是第二个问题，也是我们刚才暗示过的：你有多看重模型控制？
Now the second question, and the one that we were just hinting at, is how much do you value model control?

167
00:13:52,590 --> 00:13:57,344
这八个代理中有六个由系统为你选择模型，而只有两个允许你自带模型。
Six of these eight agents choose the model for you, while just two let you bring your own.

168
00:13:57,646 --> 00:13:59,984
实际上，模型控制大概有三个层级。
There's actually sort of three levels of model control.

169
00:14:00,206 --> 00:14:05,796
第一种是最明显的一种，即你能使用的模型就是模型提供商提供的那些。
The first is sort of the most obvious one, where whoever makes the model, those are the models that you have access to.

170
00:14:06,222 --> 00:14:09,860
所以在 Dot 中，你将使用像 GPT-6 Astra 这样的 OpenAI 模型。
So in DOT, you're going to be using OpenAI models like GPT-6 Astra.

171
00:14:10,222 --> 00:14:12,624
在 Gemini Spark 中，你将使用 Google Gemini。
In Gemini Spark, you're going to be using Google Gemini.

172
00:14:13,038 --> 00:14:15,044
在 Muse 中，你将使用 Muse Spark。
In Muse, you're going to be using Muse Spark.

173
00:14:15,438 --> 00:14:23,744
为了强调至少在现阶段，工作用户和个人用户之间确实仍有差异，我关于 Muse 有一个有趣的观察，
And to reinforce the idea that at this stage, at least, there really are still differences between work and personal users, one of the interesting observations that I've had about Muse

174
00:14:23,918 --> 00:14:30,788
它是第一款我见过的、大部分用户根本不在乎背后到底是哪个模型在驱动的 AI 产品。
is that it's the first AI product that I've ever seen where most of its users simply do not care what model is actually powering it.

175
00:14:31,150 --> 00:14:36,624
换句话说，他们在对模型控制的在意程度上处于极低的一端。
They, in other words, would be at the very extreme end of this question in terms of how little they cared about model control.

176
00:14:37,454 --> 00:14:45,344
模型控制的第二层级是它们不只使用单一厂商的模型，而是由代理构建者选择的一系列混合模型。
Level two of model control is where they don't just use a single maker's models, but have a mix that is chosen by the agent builder.

177
00:14:45,710 --> 00:14:48,784
例如，Grokbot 使用的是 Cursor 管理的混合模型。
So Grokbot, for example, uses a cursor-managed mix.

178
00:14:49,038 --> 00:14:53,424
他们显然大量依赖 Grok 模型，但可能并非全部由它提供。
They are obviously relying heavily on Grok models directly, but potentially not exclusively.

179
00:14:53,742 --> 00:14:56,836
即便如此，用户仍然无法为 Grokbot 提供自己的模型。
That said, users can still not supply the models for Grokbot.

180
00:14:57,390 --> 00:15:02,180
Instinct 也有 Instinct 系列核心模型，以及一些未命名的第三方供应商提供的模型。
Instinct also has an Instinct train core model, as well as unnamed third-party providers as well.

181
00:15:02,798 --> 00:15:08,004
第三种模型控制是「自带模型」，这显然只有 Hermes 和 OpenClaw 支持。
The third level of model control is bring your own, and that of course belongs exclusively to Hermes and OpenClaw.

182
00:15:08,430 --> 00:15:09,984
但这并不是唯一的模型问题。
But that's not the only model question.

183
00:15:10,254 --> 00:15:14,980
假设你不在乎选择自己的模型，但你确实想要一个更强大的模型。
Let's assume that you don't care about choosing your own model, but you do want a more powerful model.

184
00:15:15,470 --> 00:15:21,664
这就引出了一个问题：对你来说什么更重要？是出色的用户体验，还是最强的模型。
This brings up the question of what matters most to you, having a great user experience, or the best possible model.

185
00:15:22,094 --> 00:15:28,784
其中一部分用户体验涉及设置问题，也就是说如果你选择像 Hermes 或 OpenClaw 这样的工具，无论用户体验有多好，
Now, some of that UX is a setup question, meaning that if you're choosing something like Hermes or OpenClaw, no matter how good the user experience is,

186
00:15:29,038 --> 00:15:33,380
复杂的底层技术配置可能会让一部分用户望而却步。
the inherent technical setup required is going to cut off some full set of users.

187
00:15:33,838 --> 00:15:41,744
而在其他一些产品上，重点不在于设置，而是在于当前权衡：是追求极致精细的体验，还是追求模型的强大性能。
On some of these others, it's less about the setup and more just about the current trade-offs between how good and dialed in the experience is versus how powerful the model is.

188
00:15:42,126 --> 00:15:45,700
我这里特指光谱两端的 Muse 和 Dot。
I'm thinking specifically here about Muse on the one end of the spectrum and DOT on the other.

189
00:15:46,094 --> 00:15:53,156
Dot 刚刚发布，它以 ChatGPT 的形式嵌入在另一个应用中，但它能调用目前最强大的模型之一。
Now, DOT is still just released, it's embedded inside another app in the form of ChatGPT, but it has access to one of the most powerful models out there.

190
00:15:53,646 --> 00:16:02,052
另一方面，Muse 拥有非常成熟的、面向消费者的用户体验，但它依赖 Muse Spark，虽然这个模型并不差，但肯定比不上 GPT-6 Astra。
Muse, on the other hand, has really dialed in consumer-friendly UX, but relies on Muse Spark, which, while not a bad model at all, is certainly not GPT-6 Astra.

191
00:16:02,414 --> 00:16:09,424
这也许只是个暂时的担忧，因为你可能认为所有这些模型的性能最终都会趋同，但目前来看，它们之间仍有明显的权衡。
Now maybe this is just a temporary concern because you assume that all of these models are going to converge on capabilities, but for right now there are still real trade-offs.

192
00:16:09,838 --> 00:16:17,796
关于我设计这个测试的一点说明：为了给出具体的答案和建议，测试中的不同问题必须有不同的权重。
One thing I'll note here about how I designed the quiz, to come up with an actual answer and recommendation, the quiz had to have weighting to different questions.

193
00:16:18,286 --> 00:16:26,020
然而，有时两个答案的平均值并不能提供清晰的图景，因为两个不同的答案可能指向完全相反的方向。
However, sometimes the average of two answers isn't actually going to give a clear picture because two different answers might be pulling in two totally different directions.

194
00:16:26,510 --> 00:16:34,304
所以我在测试中加入了一个机制：当你的回答相互冲突时，测试结果会明确指出这种矛盾，
So one of the things that I built into the quiz is that when your answers pull both ways, the quiz afterwards is going to actually expose that tension,

195
00:16:34,574 --> 00:16:38,884
这样你就能更好地根据主观判断，决定哪一个方向对你来说更重要。
so you can better come to a subjective determination about which of those directions matters more to you.

196
00:16:39,438 --> 00:16:45,984
用户还需要考虑的数据流向、运行环境、谁会用它来训练，以及你如何让它“忘记”。
Another consideration for users is where your data goes, where it runs, who trains on it, and how you make it forget.

197
00:16:46,350 --> 00:16:52,836
再次强调，只有 Hermes 和 OpenClaw 允许你在自己选择的硬件上运行，其他所有工具都在别人的云端运行。
Once again, it's only two Hermes and OpenClaw that are going to let you run it on hardware you pick, all the others run on someone else's cloud.

198
00:16:53,390 --> 00:17:01,584
至于训练问题，对于 Hermes 和 OpenClaw，模型是否用你的数据进行训练并不取决于它们本身，而是取决于你选择的模型提供商。
When it comes to training, for Hermes and OpenClaw, whether the model trains on your data is not about Hermes and OpenClaw, but about the model provider that you choose.

199
00:17:01,838 --> 00:17:07,012
这意味着，如果你在意这个问题，你可以选择避开那些会使用用户数据进行训练的供应商。
Meaning, presumably, that you can choose to avoid using model providers that do train on your data if that matters to you.

200
